TAM-DEPLOY


WHAT IT DOES

tam-deploy installs a Tamlinux package assembly on a workstation through Home
Manager. Test and Run use the same package and the same installed locations;
only the evidence and the branch differ. An assembly is a tamlinux-packages
<https://github.com/greenermoose/tamlinux-packages> revision whose lock file
pins every component.

The uninstalled bundle backend also supports subsequent bundle delivery and
registered-account initialization without an existing Nix profile. See the
source checkpoint <../../docs/plans/bundle-delivery-integration.md> for its
remaining desktop-state capture gate. prepare-console <assembly> builds and
verifies the migration profile without arming root apply; console-helpers ID
writes checksum-bound bundle-on.sh and bundle-undo.sh for an exact prepared
transaction. activate-bundle ID finishes that transaction at a console.
recover ID / back returns its previous product and settings; return-nix ID
selects the originally saved Nix generation when one exists. Native
restoration is always separately approved. This source requires tam-bundle
0.13.0 for prepared-update cancellation. It does not change the current
delivery mechanism.

Per-host pins. In a fleet configuration (hosts/<hostname>/host.json), a host
can record its own assembly revision in hosts/<hostname>/state/assembly, one
40-character revision per file. The consumer flake reads that file for the
host, so machines on different versions never rewrite a shared lock. When the
file exists, tam-deploy reads, pins, restores and commits it in place of
flake.lock. A candidate build stages the file only for the duration of the
build. Hosts without the file keep using the configuration lock.

  Command: status
  Effect: Show the pinned assembly, its component revisions, the installed
  shell revision, the stage and the generation; compare required endpoints
  with the exact selected package outputs, rejecting missing/broken links and
  mixed assemblies.

  Command: status --json
  Effect: Separately report profile, receipt, lock, endpoints, running shell
  identity and the transaction. Disagreement returns a nonzero exit status.

  Command: preflight <assembly> [--json]
  Effect: Require a coherent baseline, build the exact target without changing
  the consumer lock, and run registered read-only checks on candidate and
  recovery generations. Print the built generation and detected home-file
  conflicts as JSON.

  Command: conflicts <generation> [--json]
  Effect: List unmanaged $HOME files, directories or non-Home Manager symlinks
  that the generation's home-files would overwrite, along with their
  <path>.pre-<version> backup targets. Read-only; always exits 0 when the
  generation exists.

  Command: reconcile [--digest HASH]
  Effect: Plan a correction only when the profile pointer is the sole
  mismatch. The digest-bound execution changes that pointer using Nix; it does
  not activate, restore settings or invent a receipt. Unknown runtime or mixed
  endpoints are refused.

  Command: init [revision] [--test]
  Effect: First installation on a registered local host without an existing
  generation. Require all components on main (or test with --test), build the
  exact consumer generation, activate and verify its package endpoints. Works
  from a text session without a Home Manager CLI. Records no previous
  generation; never promotes branches or enables graphical login.

  Command: test [revision]
  Effect: Require the assembly and each pinned component to be on test. Pin
  the revision (default: the head of test) in the configuration lock, switch
  Home Manager, restart the shell if active, verify the installed paths and
  commit the pin. Activation or verification failure attempts to restore the
  lock and previous generation.

  Command: run [revision] [--untested]
  Effect: Make an assembly the daily one and move it onto main. Without a
  revision: promote the installed Test to main without rebuilding, or report
  that a main commit is already running. With a revision (any
  tamlinux-packages commit, full or abbreviated): install it if needed, then
  fast-forward main; a main commit (including an older one) moves no branch. A
  commit, or a component it pins, that is only on develop or another branch is
  untested and needs --untested; it then moves test too. A revision main
  cannot fast-forward to is refused.

  Command: back
  Effect: Activate the previous generation, verify its endpoints against the
  recorded assembly, and restore its pin.

  Command: follow <revision> [--transaction ID --expect-baseline SHA256]
  Effect: Install an accepted assembly without stage promotion; an upgrade
  caller can bind it to the confirmed starting state.

  Command: recover ID
  Effect: Recover only the current identified deployment transaction to its
  saved baseline, using local generation and package verification data.

The rollback pin identifies the assembly actually running, using the
deployment record for the active generation. A prepared but inactive consumer
lock is not the rollback assembly. Without a matching record, the current lock
must match the installed source revision; otherwise deployment stops before
activation. Back identifies its return pin from the active deployment record
too, so a staged consumer lock cannot pair the old generation with a newer
assembly. It records the restored generation and components for subsequent
deployments.

Before Test, Run or Back, the profile, receipt, selected assembly, generation
markers, package endpoints and running shell must agree. A staged lock or
stale receipt requires explicit reconciliation before another deployment.

Test builds before changing the live lock, then invokes the built generation's
activate script with its default Home Manager driver. That script runs its
prerequisite checks before moving the profile at writeBoundary. The Home
Manager CLI's switch ordering, which moves the profile first, is not used.
Consumer-specific read-only helpers can be registered in
system/activation-preflight.json as validators with scriptName and
repository-relative source. The named immutable Python helper is located in
each generation's activation script. Only registered helpers run; full
activation and arbitrary DRY_RUN hooks are never used for preparation.

$XDG_STATE_HOME/tamlinux/deploy-transaction.json records baseline, target and
phase before mutation. Before running the generation's activate script,
tam-deploy checks for unmanaged $HOME paths that home-files would overwrite,
renames each to <path>.pre-<version> (or .pre-tamlinux when no version stamp
is present) while setting HOME_MANAGER_BACKUP_EXT, and records backedUp in the
transaction and deployment state. If <path>.pre-<version> already exists,
activation is refused before any file is moved. Activation failure or
tam-deploy back restores the moved .pre-<version> files alongside the previous
lock and generation, checking endpoints, generation markers and runtime before
claiming recovery. Both the original and recovery errors survive a partial
failure. An incomplete transaction blocks another attempt. Status exposes it
for explicit recovery; deleting the file is not a recovery procedure.

Promote a candidate from develop to test (a fast-forward push) before
tam-deploy test; tam-deploy never resolves a moving branch at build time.


INSTALL

    make check
    make install   # honours PREFIX and DESTDIR


CONFIGURATION

  Variable: TAMLINUX_CONFIG_REPO
  Default: The one checkout under ~/Code/tamlinux/ whose flake.lock has a
  tamlinux-packages input

  Variable: TAMLINUX_HOME_CONFIGURATION
  Default: The registered local user@hostname; a different host's output is
  refused. Legacy configurations without hosts/ retain the current user name.

  Variable: TAMLINUX_WORKSPACE
  Default: ~/Code/tamlinux, holding clones of tamlinux-packages and each
  component

  Variable: TAMLINUX_PACKAGES_INPUT
  Default: tamlinux-packages, the configuration's flake input

  Variable: TAMLINUX_GITHUB_OWNER
  Default: greenermoose

The configuration's tamlinux-packages input must name the repository without a
revision (for example github:greenermoose/tamlinux-packages); the lock file
holds the exact revision.

test and run refuse while the configuration has uncommitted or untracked
changes, so every generation has an identifiable configuration commit. Two
kinds of path are exempt: the deployment records (system/bom.json,
system/plugin-runs) and the files listed in system/live-paths, one
repository-relative path per line (# comments allowed). List only files the
running session writes through a Home Manager live (out-of-store) link, such
as the shell's settings.json: their content is not part of a generation. Their
SHA-256 at deployment is recorded as livePaths in the deployment state.


EXACT ENDPOINT VERIFICATION

The command evaluates packages.<system> at the committed assembly revision
through its local Git history and compares resolved endpoints with those exact
store outputs. It checks the shell, every selected plugin, every product
command, tam, tam-file-select, and the menu/tam-man endpoints when the
assembly provides them. Missing files, broken links, unexpected plugins and
old packages fail verification. The installed deployment-selection.json
records intentional plugin subsets; its IDs and product revision must agree
with the assembly. Older assemblies without that record are checked against
their full plugin set. Nix evaluation must succeed; no name-pattern fallback
declares a match.


FIRST INSTALLATION FROM A CONSOLE

Register the machine in the consumer configuration before installing. Keep a
verified native-base recovery snapshot and boot medium: there is no previous
Home Manager generation. Review conflicting account files before activation.
The command can be entered through the exact assembly's command package:

    nix shell github:greenermoose/tamlinux-packages/<assembly-revision>#tamlinux-commands \
      --command tam-deploy init <assembly-revision>

Only an assembly whose components are already on main is accepted by default.
An explicitly authorized Test installation uses --test; it requires the
assembly and components on test and never promotes them. init installs the
package and records endpoints; physical console and graphical acceptance
remain separate. It never starts a display manager or compositor.

Build failure restores the previous lock before any account activation. An
activation/verification failure retains first-install.json beside the local
deployment record with the attempted assembly, generation and previous lock.
It refuses an automatic retry rather than claiming recovery without a prior
generation. Inspect partial links and restore from the native-base recovery
point before retrying. Ordinary upgrades continue to require a known rollback
generation. From a text login with no active graphical session, activation and
rollback do not start the desktop or require shell IPC. If a graphical session
is active, the shell is restarted and checked. An unavailable user service
manager or ambiguous session state stops the operation. When the graphical
target is active, deployment checks every non-manager login session belonging
to the user for a lock, even when invoked from a different text console. It
checks again before restarting the shell and before automatic generation
recovery. Locked or uninspectable sessions stop the operation; a lock that
appears during activation can leave a partial deployment requiring inspection.
These checks cannot prevent a session from locking between inspection and the
next operation.

If recovery fails after an installation error, or recording fails after an
activation, the generation, consumer lock and deployment record may disagree.
The same is true if rollback fails after activation is attempted. Inspect
tam-deploy status, the active generation and the consumer lock before
retrying; the error does not claim that the prior state was restored. Ordinary
rollback verifies the previous assembly's endpoints using its recorded
assembly. Identified transaction recovery instead retains those immutable
package identities before mutation and uses them without Nix evaluation,
component checkouts or network discovery.


IDENTIFIED TRANSACTION RECOVERY

Every command that can change deployment state takes the same nonblocking
deploy.lock, including preparation because it may temporarily stage a host
pin. A transaction has a stable ID and records the local host, user, consumer
configuration, baseline assembly/generation, exact original pin, deployment
receipt and baseline endpoint identities. Current and historical JSON records
are published atomically under the state directory.

recover ID refuses stale/foreign IDs, missing generations, unrelated pins or
profiles, and later pin edits. Legacy transactions lacking recovery identity
remain readable but cannot be recovered by guessing. A baseline already
restored by automatic recovery is checked without another activation. Partial
activation or recording can recover to the saved baseline rather than the
current deployment's ordinary previous generation. Recovery verifies actual
package endpoints, profile, source marker and applicable shell runtime before
recording success. It does not move stage branches.

Home-file backup intent is recorded before renaming. Recovery restores only
backups actually present, preserves later user edits and reports their
conflicts as incomplete recovery. Original error and recovery error remain
available. Recovery still needs the retained generation and local consumer; it
is not a replacement for native/boot snapshots or a full settings checkpoint.
An interrupted pin write whose exact target bytes were not recorded requires
manual inspection; the tool does not guess that an edited lock is safe.
